From: Jon Pretty <jon.pretty@propensive.com>
Date: Mon, 3 Aug 2026 16:29:04 +0200
Subject: Splice any type with a Spreadable instance into a vararg position
MIME-Version: 1.0
Content-Type: text/plain; charset=UTF-8
Content-Transfer-Encoding: 8bit

Squashed from 2 commits, oldest first:

* Splice any type with a Spreadable instance into a vararg position

A `T*` parameter is a `Seq[T]` underneath, so only a `Seq` or an `Array` can be
spliced with `xs*`. An abstraction that hides one — an opaque type alias over a
`Vector`, as Soundness's prelude uses throughout — cannot be, and neither can an
unrelated type that merely has elements to offer.

`scala.Spreadable[T]` grants that permission for one type, and only at splice
positions. It is not a `Conversion`: the typer consults it nowhere else, so the
abstraction stays opaque under member selection, which is what made a companion
`Conversion[Series[e], Seq[e]]` unusable — that also resurrects `head`, `apply`
and the rest of the `Seq` surface the alias exists to hide.

The instance names the `Seq`/`Array` it spreads to as its `Out`. Where the
representation already is one — the opaque-alias case — the splice elaborates
to a cast and `spread` is never called, so nothing is allocated and nothing is
added at erasure; the instance written inside the alias's own file, where the
alias is transparent, is just the identity. Otherwise `spread` converts, which
is what admits a type unrelated to the collections.

The cast targets the representation, never the instance's `Out`: an instance
naming an `Out` its type does not actually have would otherwise be a
ClassCastException at runtime rather than a compile error.

Elaboration stays on the failure path of `typedWildcardStarArgExpr`, so every
splice that already typechecks is untouched, as is behaviour inside the alias's
defining scope. The retype and the implicit search run in a fresh typer state
that is committed only when the expression really was spreadable, so an
erroneous splice — a mistyped one inside a quote, pre-typed during speculative
overload resolution — cannot leak type variables or quote-hole registrations
into the enclosing state.

`Out` is deliberately unbounded. `<: Seq[?] | Array[?]` would be the honest
bound but cannot be written: an opaque alias over an `Array` — `IArray` itself —
does not conform to it outside its defining scope, which is the very case this
serves. The compiler checks at the splice site instead, seeing through opaque
aliases as it goes.

`Spreadable` is `@experimental`, which is the whole gate: instances cannot be
written outside experimental mode, so no separate language import is needed.

Known limitation: under `-Ycc-new` the instance cannot be keyed on a type
capturing the root capability — `Spreadable[Vector[() => Unit]]` is rejected
with "type variable T cannot be instantiated ... captures the root capability
`any`". That is a general capture-checking restriction on type arguments,
reproducible with a bare `trait Box[T]`. Element types capturing a named
capability are unaffected.

Replaces the `spliceopaque` patch, which pierced any opaque alias over a
`Seq`/`Array` unconditionally, with no way for a library to opt out.

Developed with LLM assistance (Claude, Anthropic). Verified against a compiler
built from this change: aliases over `Vector` and over `IArray`, an unrelated
class needing a real conversion, and callee type-parameter inference through the
splice; an alias without an instance gets the stock error and `.head` on one
with an instance is still not a member; bytecode shows no `spread` call for the
alias cases; and both of spliceopaque's crash reproductions error cleanly.

Enabled by `-Z:spreadable-varargs`; without it, upstream's code runs at every site this
patch touches.

* Ship Spreadable in the supplementary proscala-library jar

The standard library additions of the fork live under library-proscala/src and
are built into their own jar (proscala-library, plus a Scala.js variant), so the
scala-library and scalajs-scalalib jars are byte-identical to what upstream
builds from the same tree. The compiler looks the class up by name and runs
without it.

